1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,659
This is STAT 331, video 20. It covers Lecture 10: interactions and non-linear terms.

2
00:00:08,109 --> 00:00:14,573
This video prepares you for three question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:15,023 --> 00:00:21,465
Type A: write the line of each group in a model with interaction terms, and interpret each beta.

4
00:00:21,915 --> 00:00:33,094
Type B: test an effect, or give a confidence interval for it. Use a t test for one linear combination, and an F test for a joint null hypothesis.

5
00:00:33,544 --> 00:00:43,869
Type C: polynomial terms, such as x squared. Find the effect of x at a given x, compare models, and use the hierarchical principle.

6
00:00:44,319 --> 00:00:59,253
Section 1. First, a short recap of Lecture 9. These are the fishermen mercury data. MeHg is the methyl mercury in a hair sample. The colour shows fishpart, the parts of fish that a person eats.

7
00:00:59,703 --> 00:01:17,217
Lecture 9 puts fishpart into the model with indicator variables. M i is 1 if subject i eats muscle tissue only, and 0 if not. M W i and W i do the same for two other groups. Group N is the baseline.

8
00:01:17,667 --> 00:01:21,934
The fitted model gives four parallel lines, one for each group.

9
00:01:22,384 --> 00:01:28,720
To test one coefficient, or one linear combination of coefficients, we use a t test.

10
00:01:29,170 --> 00:01:43,271
To test a joint null hypothesis, beta star equals 0, we use the F test. Its degrees of freedom are q and n minus p plus 1. Question type B uses both tests again.

11
00:01:43,721 --> 00:01:50,569
Slide 9 writes the model with shorter names. The table shows the three indicators for each group.

12
00:01:51,019 --> 00:01:58,849
For group N, all three indicators are 0. N is the baseline category, also called the referent category.

13
00:01:59,299 --> 00:02:10,008
Put the indicator values into the model. Each group gets its own intercept. For example, group M has the intercept beta 0 plus beta M.

14
00:02:10,458 --> 00:02:22,981
But all four lines have the same slope, beta 1. One more kilogram changes the mean MeHg by the same amount in every group. This is the common slope model.

15
00:02:23,431 --> 00:02:33,073
The problem: in the data, the slope can be different in each group. We need a term that gives each group its own slope. That term is an interaction.

16
00:02:33,523 --> 00:02:50,526
Section 2, question type A. Slide 11 gives each group its own slope. Look at the design matrix X. Each row is one subject. Here is one subject from each group. The first five columns are the columns of the common slope model.

17
00:02:50,976 --> 00:03:05,035
Now add three product columns, in orange. Each one is weight i times one indicator. A product of two covariates is an interaction term. A term with one covariate alone is a main effect.

18
00:03:05,485 --> 00:03:20,311
A product column equals the weight in the rows of its group. In all other rows, it is 0. For example, the column weight i times M i is 73 for the subject in group M, and 0 for the others.

19
00:03:20,761 --> 00:03:34,201
Each column has its own coefficient. Beta 1 M, beta 1 M W, and beta 1 W are the coefficients of the interaction terms. The model has eight coefficients.

20
00:03:34,651 --> 00:03:39,281
Method card A solves every question of type A. It has four steps.

21
00:03:39,731 --> 00:03:49,565
Step 1: fix the other covariate. Put each indicator to 0 or 1. For a continuous covariate x 2, put x 2 to one value.

22
00:03:50,015 --> 00:04:04,309
Step 2: collect the terms. Write the mean as an intercept plus a slope times x. Each group gets one line. The picture shows two lines, for groups N and M W. It uses made-up numbers.

23
00:04:04,759 --> 00:04:19,265
Step 3: name each beta. The baseline group gives the baseline intercept and slope. A main effect is an intercept difference. An interaction coefficient is a slope difference: the orange part on the right.

24
00:04:19,715 --> 00:04:32,323
Step 4: say it in the units of the problem, for the mean of y. An intercept is the mean at x equals 0. It has a practical meaning only if x equals 0 is near the data.

25
00:04:32,773 --> 00:04:44,720
Here is the question of slides 11 to 13. Pause the video and try it. Write the mean of MeHg when N i is 1, and when M W i is 1.

26
00:04:45,170 --> 00:05:01,618
Step 1 of card A: fix the group. For group N, the indicators M, M W and W are 0. So their three terms and the three product terms drop. The mean is beta 0 plus beta 1 times weight: the black line.

27
00:05:02,068 --> 00:05:15,743
For group M W, M W i is 1, and the other two indicators are 0. Two extra terms stay, in orange: beta M W, and beta 1 M W times weight.

28
00:05:16,193 --> 00:05:32,107
Step 2: collect the terms. The intercept is beta 0 plus beta M W. The slope is beta 1 plus beta 1 M W. So group M W has its own intercept and its own slope: the teal line.

29
00:05:32,557 --> 00:05:48,728
Step 3: name the betas. Beta M W is the intercept of M W minus the intercept of N. Beta 1 M W is the slope of M W minus the slope of N: the orange part of the bar on the right.

30
00:05:49,178 --> 00:05:56,709
Groups M and W follow the same steps, with M i equal to 1 or W i equal to 1.

31
00:05:57,159 --> 00:06:12,583
Slides 14 and 15 ask: what is beta 1, and what is beta 1 M W. Pause the video and try it. Take group N. Compare weight a plus 1 with weight a. The difference of the two means is beta 1.

32
00:06:13,033 --> 00:06:27,710
Put in the N line: beta 0 plus beta 1 times a plus 1, minus beta 0 plus beta 1 a. This is beta 1. So beta 1 is the slope of the N line: the black step on the left.

33
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:37,589
Do the same for group M W. The difference of the two means is beta 1 plus beta 1 M W, the slope of the teal line.

34
00:06:38,039 --> 00:06:48,578
Line 3 minus line 1: beta 1 M W equals the M W slope minus the N slope. The bar on the right shows it in orange.

35
00:06:49,028 --> 00:06:58,948
Each slope is a difference of two means. So beta 1 M W is a difference in differences. If it is 0, the two lines are parallel.

36
00:06:59,398 --> 00:07:05,094
The lecture R code fits both models to the mercury data. These are the data again.

37
00:07:05,544 --> 00:07:09,064
The common slope model gives four parallel lines.

38
00:07:09,514 --> 00:07:21,461
The interaction model gives each group its own slope, so the lines are not parallel. Step 2 of card A: the table gives the slope of each group, computed from the data.

39
00:07:21,911 --> 00:07:37,356
Step 3: beta 1 M W hat is the M W slope minus the N slope. 0.1187 minus 0.0049 is 0.1137.

40
00:07:37,806 --> 00:07:48,046
Step 4: read the group sizes. Group N has only 10 subjects, and group W has 9. So the slopes of these two lines are uncertain.

41
00:07:48,496 --> 00:08:03,387
The next example uses the real estate data of slide 16. Each row is one house that was sold. SalePrice is the price in thousands of dollars. SqrFeet is the floor area in square feet.

42
00:08:03,837 --> 00:08:11,367
Air is an indicator variable. It is 1 if the house has air conditioning, A C. It is 0 if not.

43
00:08:11,817 --> 00:08:23,039
The plot shows all 521 houses. Blue points have air conditioning, and black points do not. 433 houses have air conditioning.

44
00:08:23,489 --> 00:08:41,345
Slide 16 fits three models. Use steps 1 and 2 of card A on each. Model M1 has SqrFeet and Air. Put Air equal to 1: the intercept is beta 0 plus beta 2, and the slope stays beta 1. So the lines are parallel.

45
00:08:41,795 --> 00:08:57,987
Model M2 has SqrFeet and the product SqrFeet times Air, but not Air alone. Put Air equal to 1: the slope is beta 1 plus beta 2, and the intercept stays beta 0. The two lines start at the same point.

46
00:08:58,437 --> 00:09:11,642
Model M3 has Air, SqrFeet, and their product. Put Air equal to 1: the intercept is beta 0 plus beta 1, and the slope is beta 2 plus beta 3. Both differ.

47
00:09:12,092 --> 00:09:29,052
M3 has both main effects and the interaction. In R, Air star SqrFeet asks for these three terms. Later, the hierarchical principle says why M3 is the usual choice. The lines in the panels are fitted to the data.

48
00:09:29,502 --> 00:09:37,075
Slide 17 shows the data with the fitted lines of M1 and M3. We drew it again from the data.

49
00:09:37,525 --> 00:09:47,552
The solid lines are M1. They are parallel. The A C line is higher by 26.63 thousand dollars at every floor area.

50
00:09:48,002 --> 00:09:55,426
The dotted lines are M3. Each group has its own intercept and its own slope, computed from the data.

51
00:09:55,876 --> 00:10:09,103
In M3, the A C slope, 0.1608, is larger. For small houses, the two lines are close. For large houses, A C houses have a much higher mean price.

52
00:10:09,553 --> 00:10:29,073
Here is the question for model M3. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card A: put Air equal to 0. The mean is beta 0 hat plus beta 2 hat x: minus 3.22 plus 0.1049 x. The black line shows it.

53
00:10:29,523 --> 00:10:41,021
Put Air equal to 1, and collect. The intercept is beta 0 hat plus beta 1 hat. The slope is beta 2 hat plus beta 3 hat. The blue line shows it.

54
00:10:41,471 --> 00:10:57,834
With numbers: minus 3.22 minus 78.87 is minus 82.09. 0.1049 plus 0.0559 is 0.1608.

55
00:10:58,284 --> 00:11:07,372
The gap between the two lines is line 2 minus line 1: beta 1 hat plus beta 3 hat x. It depends on the floor area.

56
00:11:07,822 --> 00:11:23,886
At 2000 square feet: 0.0559 times 2000 is 111.8. Minus 78.87 plus 111.8 is 32.9 thousand dollars.

57
00:11:24,336 --> 00:11:39,099
Step 3 of card A: name each coefficient. Beta 0 hat, minus 3.22, is the mean price of a house without A C and with 0 square feet. It is the black line at x equals 0.

58
00:11:39,549 --> 00:11:52,349
Beta 1 hat, minus 78.87, is the difference in mean price, A C minus no A C, at 0 square feet: the gap of the two lines at x equals 0.

59
00:11:52,799 --> 00:12:08,692
Beta 2 hat, 0.1049, is the change in mean price per square foot for houses without A C. Over 1000 square feet, that is 104.9 thousand dollars: the black rise.

60
00:12:09,142 --> 00:12:26,529
Beta 3 hat, 0.0559, is the A C slope minus the no A C slope. The A C slope is 0.1608: 160.8 thousand dollars per 1000 square feet, the blue rise.

61
00:12:26,979 --> 00:12:41,784
Step 4: no house has 0 square feet. So beta 0 hat and beta 1 hat alone have no practical meaning. Report the gap, beta 1 hat plus beta 3 hat x, at a floor area in the data.

62
00:12:42,234 --> 00:12:58,810
Now a tutorial question of type A. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 3, question 1 e, fits salary on district size and experience, with interactions. The output gives six estimates, beta 0 to beta 5.

63
00:12:59,260 --> 00:13:13,660
Step 1 of card A: for size 1, both size indicators are 0. The line is 24079.59 plus 662.79 times experience.

64
00:13:14,110 --> 00:13:39,774
For size 2, size f 2 is 1. Step 2: collect. The intercept is 24079.59 plus 5347.29, which is 29426.88. The slope is 662.79 minus 186.99, which is 475.80.

65
00:13:40,224 --> 00:14:01,323
For size 3, the intercept is 24079.59 plus 12004.24, which is 36083.83. The slope is 662.79 minus 518.91, which is 143.88.

66
00:14:01,773 --> 00:14:13,463
Step 3 of card A: name each beta. Beta 0 is the mean salary in size 1 districts with experience 0: the black line at x equals 0.

67
00:14:13,913 --> 00:14:23,748
Beta 1 is the difference in mean salary, size 2 minus size 1, at experience 0: the orange gap at x equals 0.

68
00:14:24,198 --> 00:14:31,067
Beta 2 is the difference in mean salary, size 3 minus size 1, at experience 0.

69
00:14:31,517 --> 00:14:48,307
Beta 3 is the change in mean salary for one more year of experience, in size 1 districts. The black step shows 5 years: 5 times 662.79, plus 3314 dollars.

70
00:14:48,757 --> 00:15:12,586
Beta 4 is the slope of size 2 minus the slope of size 1: 475.80 minus 662.79. It is a difference in differences. The blue step shows 5 years in size 2: 5 times 475.80, plus 2379 dollars.

71
00:15:13,036 --> 00:15:33,473
Beta 5 is the slope of size 3 minus the slope of size 1: 143.88 minus 662.79. The purple step shows 5 years in size 3: 5 times 143.88, plus 719 dollars.

72
00:15:33,923 --> 00:15:46,126
Step 4: the output does not give the range of experience. If experience 0 is far from the data, beta 0, beta 1 and beta 2 have no practical meaning.

73
00:15:46,576 --> 00:15:55,963
Slides 20 and 21 take two continuous covariates and their product. Pause the video and try it. How do we interpret beta 1?

74
00:15:56,413 --> 00:16:06,546
Step 1 of card A: fix x 2 at one value. The mean is a function of x 1. For x 2 equal to 0, the black line shows it.

75
00:16:06,996 --> 00:16:22,505
Step 2: collect in x 1. The intercept is beta 0 plus beta 2 x 2. The slope is beta 1 plus beta 3 x 2. Both change with x 2. The blue line is for x 2 equal to 1.

76
00:16:22,955 --> 00:16:27,179
Now increase x 1 by one unit, and subtract the two means.

77
00:16:27,629 --> 00:16:32,920
Put in line 2 for both means. The purple line is for x 2 equal to 2.

78
00:16:33,370 --> 00:16:44,271
The intercept terms cancel, and x 1 cancels. The change is beta 1 plus beta 3 x 2. So the effect of x 1 depends on x 2.

79
00:16:44,721 --> 00:16:57,159
Step 3: put x 2 equal to 0. The change is beta 1. So beta 1 is the change in mean y for a one-unit increase in x 1, when x 2 is 0.

80
00:16:57,609 --> 00:17:09,065
Practice question 2 asks for beta 3. Pause the video and try it. Let mu of x 1, x 2 be the mean of y. The table shows the mean at four points.

81
00:17:09,515 --> 00:17:22,805
In the row x 2 equals b, increase x 1 from a to a plus 1. From line 5 of the last slide, the mean changes by beta 1 plus beta 3 b: the orange arrow.

82
00:17:23,255 --> 00:17:33,794
In the row x 2 equals b plus 1, the same increase changes the mean by beta 1 plus beta 3 times b plus 1: the purple arrow.

83
00:17:34,244 --> 00:17:45,700
Line 2 minus line 1. Beta 1 cancels, and beta 3 times b cancels. What is left is beta 3. So beta 3 is a difference of differences.

84
00:17:46,150 --> 00:17:54,961
In words: beta 3 is how much the effect of one more unit of x 1 changes when x 2 increases by one unit.

85
00:17:55,411 --> 00:18:07,379
Practice question 3 uses the real estate data. The model has SqrFeet, Bedrooms, and their product. Pause the video and try it. The table is computed from the data.

86
00:18:07,829 --> 00:18:19,605
Step 3 of card A: name each beta. Beta 0 hat, minus 209.26, is the mean price at 0 square feet and 0 bedrooms.

87
00:18:20,055 --> 00:18:37,015
Beta 1 hat, 0.2269, is the change in mean price per square foot, for a house with 0 bedrooms. Beta 2 hat, 30.22, is the change in mean price for one more bedroom, at 0 square feet.

88
00:18:37,465 --> 00:18:55,641
Beta 3 hat is minus 0.0159. Each extra bedroom lowers the slope per square foot by 0.0159 thousand dollars. Its t value, minus 4.11, is large in absolute value.

89
00:18:56,091 --> 00:19:08,933
Step 4: no house has 0 square feet, and no house in the data has 0 bedrooms. So beta 0, beta 1 and beta 2 hat alone describe a house outside the data.

90
00:19:09,383 --> 00:19:26,237
Now plot the fitted line for a given number of bedrooms, k. Steps 1 and 2 of card A: put Bedrooms equal to k, and collect. The intercept is beta 0 hat plus beta 2 hat k. The slope is beta 1 hat plus beta 3 hat k.

91
00:19:26,687 --> 00:19:36,735
k equals 0: the intercept is minus 209.26, and the slope is 0.2269. The black line.

92
00:19:37,185 --> 00:20:00,758
k equals 1: the intercept is minus 209.265 plus 30.222, which is minus 179.04. The slope is 0.22695 minus 0.01592, which is 0.2110. The blue line is a little flatter.

93
00:20:01,208 --> 00:20:26,061
k equals 7: 7 times 30.222 is 211.554, so the intercept is 2.29. 7 times 0.01592 is 0.11144, so the slope is 0.1155. The pink line is much flatter. The interaction changes the slope.

94
00:20:26,511 --> 00:20:39,354
Step 4: only 3 houses have 7 bedrooms, all with 2812 square feet or more. No house has 0 bedrooms, so that line is an extrapolation.

95
00:20:39,804 --> 00:20:51,409
Section 3, question type B. With interactions, the effect of a covariate is often a sum of betas. Method card B tests such an effect. It has five steps.

96
00:20:51,859 --> 00:21:03,571
Step 1: write the effect from the question. It is one linear combination, c transpose beta, or a set of betas that are all 0 under the null hypothesis.

97
00:21:04,021 --> 00:21:16,224
Step 2: one combination: use a t test, or a confidence interval. Several betas equal to 0 together: use the F test. q is the number of these betas.

98
00:21:16,674 --> 00:21:34,615
Step 3: compute. The estimate is c transpose beta hat. Its squared standard error is the sum of c j squared times V j j, plus 2 times the sum of c j c k times V j k. The V values come from the vcov output.

99
00:21:35,065 --> 00:21:47,311
Step 4: compare the statistic with the t or F quantile, or use the p-value. A confidence interval is the estimate plus or minus t times the standard error.

100
00:21:47,761 --> 00:21:50,726
Step 5: conclude in the words of the problem.

101
00:21:51,176 --> 00:22:10,888
The exercise of slides 18 and 19 is a type B question. 59 students rated the course. I of Fast i is 1 if the student finds the pace fast. I of Grad i is 1 if the student considers grad school. The model has both indicators and their product.

102
00:22:11,338 --> 00:22:15,626
The slide gives the R output. coef gives the four estimates.

103
00:22:16,076 --> 00:22:24,460
vcov gives the estimated variance matrix of beta hat: sigma hat squared, times X transpose X inverse.

104
00:22:24,910 --> 00:22:29,625
The degrees of freedom are 59 minus 4, which is 55.

105
00:22:30,075 --> 00:22:45,179
Put the 0 and 1 values into the model. Each cell of the table has its own mean. A student with both indicators equal to 1 has the mean beta 0, plus beta 1, plus beta 2, plus beta 3.

106
00:22:45,629 --> 00:22:52,946
Question 1. Is the mean course rating associated with perceived lecture pace? Pause the video and try it.

107
00:22:53,396 --> 00:23:04,255
Step 1 of card B: write the effect. For students with Grad equal to 0, a fast pace changes the mean by beta 1: the top row of the table.

108
00:23:04,705 --> 00:23:12,982
For students with Grad equal to 1, a fast pace changes the mean by beta 1 plus beta 3: the bottom row.

109
00:23:13,432 --> 00:23:28,451
Pace has no association with the mean rating only when both changes are 0, so when beta 1 and beta 3 are 0. Step 2: two betas together, so we use the F test with q equal to 2.

110
00:23:28,901 --> 00:23:45,434
Step 3 of card B: compute F. The vcov output already includes sigma hat squared. So F is beta star hat transpose, times the inverse of the 2 by 2 block of vcov, times beta star hat, over q.

111
00:23:45,884 --> 00:23:53,308
Take the block of the Fast and Fast:Grad rows and columns of vcov: the green and orange cells.

112
00:23:53,758 --> 00:24:16,222
The determinant of the block: 0.09073 times 0.18343, minus 0.09073 squared. That is 0.016643 minus 0.008232, which is 0.008411.

113
00:24:16,672 --> 00:24:25,696
Invert the 2 by 2 block. Swap the two diagonal entries, change the sign of the other two, and divide by the determinant.

114
00:24:26,146 --> 00:24:40,098
Multiply out beta star hat transpose, times this matrix, times beta star hat. We keep the division by the determinant for later. Both estimates are negative, so the middle term is positive.

115
00:24:40,548 --> 00:24:54,201
The three terms are 0.0407, 0.0477 and 0.0283. Their sum is 0.1167.

116
00:24:54,651 --> 00:25:12,998
Divide by the determinant, 0.008411: 13.87. Divide by q, which is 2. F is 6.937. The plot shows the F density with 2 and 55 degrees of freedom, and the observed F.

117
00:25:13,448 --> 00:25:35,037
Step 4: the 95 percent quantile of F with 2 and 55 degrees of freedom is 3.165. The observed F is larger, and the p-value is 0.0021. Step 5: we reject the null hypothesis. Mean rating is associated with pace.

118
00:25:35,487 --> 00:25:56,501
Question 2. Test for association between pace and rating among students who consider grad school. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: for these students, Grad is 1, so the pace effect is beta 1 plus beta 3. The null hypothesis is beta 1 plus beta 3 equals 0.

119
00:25:56,951 --> 00:26:04,673
Step 2: this is one linear combination, so we use a t test. The vector c is 0, 1, 0, 1.

120
00:26:05,123 --> 00:26:16,622
Step 3: the estimate is minus 0.4709 minus 0.5585, which is minus 1.0294.

121
00:26:17,072 --> 00:26:40,261
Its variance is the variance of beta 1 hat, plus the variance of beta 3 hat, plus 2 times their covariance: the green and orange cells. 0.09073 plus 0.18343 minus 2 times 0.09073 is 0.0927.

122
00:26:40,711 --> 00:26:55,623
The standard error is the square root, 0.3045. t is minus 1.0294 over 0.3045, which is minus 3.381.

123
00:26:56,073 --> 00:27:11,753
Step 4: the absolute value of t, 3.381, is larger than 2.004, the t quantile with 55 degrees of freedom. The p-value is 0.0013.

124
00:27:12,203 --> 00:27:22,102
Step 5: we reject the null hypothesis. Among students who consider grad school, those who find the pace fast give a lower mean rating.

125
00:27:22,552 --> 00:27:38,317
Question 3. Now take the students who do not consider grad school. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: Grad is 0, so the pace effect is beta 1 alone. The null hypothesis is beta 1 equals 0.

126
00:27:38,767 --> 00:27:41,925
Step 2: one coefficient, so we use a t test.

127
00:27:42,375 --> 00:28:02,385
Step 3: the standard error is the square root of 0.09073, which is 0.3012. t is minus 0.4709 over 0.3012, which is minus 1.563.

128
00:28:02,835 --> 00:28:12,073
Step 4: the absolute value of t is smaller than 2.004. The p-value is 0.124.

129
00:28:12,523 --> 00:28:21,760
Step 5: we do not reject the null hypothesis. The data do not show an association between pace and rating in this group.

130
00:28:22,210 --> 00:28:32,301
Exercise 2 rejects and exercise 3 does not. So the effect of pace depends on grad school interest. This is what an interaction means.

131
00:28:32,751 --> 00:28:50,713
Items 4 and 5 ask for a mean rating with a 95 percent confidence interval. They name a morning or afternoon section, but the model has only Fast and Grad. So we use the same method for the four cells of the model. Pause the video and try it.

132
00:28:51,163 --> 00:29:04,518
Step 1 of card B: write the mean. For students who find the pace fast and do not consider grad school, the mean is beta 0 plus beta 1. So c is 1, 1, 0, 0.

133
00:29:04,968 --> 00:29:17,832
Step 2: one combination, so the interval is the estimate plus or minus t times the standard error. The t quantile with 55 degrees of freedom is 2.004.

134
00:29:18,282 --> 00:29:29,482
Step 3: the estimate is 4.1852 minus 0.4709, which is 3.7143.

135
00:29:29,932 --> 00:29:51,884
The variance is V 0 0 plus V 1 1 plus 2 V 0 1: the green and orange cells of vcov. 0.01868 plus 0.09073 minus 2 times 0.01868 is 0.07205.

136
00:29:52,334 --> 00:30:03,150
The standard error is 0.2684. 2.004 times 0.2684 is 0.538.

137
00:30:03,600 --> 00:30:23,845
Step 4: the interval is 3.714 plus or minus 0.538: 3.176 to 4.252. The plot shows all four cells, computed with the same steps. The lowest mean, 3.5, is for Fast 1 and Grad 1.

138
00:30:24,295 --> 00:30:42,514
A tutorial question of type B. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 3, question 1 f. Step 1 of card B: the slope in size 1 is beta 3, and in size 2 it is beta 3 plus beta 4. They are equal when beta 4 is 0.

139
00:30:42,964 --> 00:30:54,975
Step 2: one coefficient, so a t test. The model has 6 coefficients, so the degrees of freedom are 325 minus 6, which is 319.

140
00:30:55,425 --> 00:31:08,822
Step 3: t is the estimate over its standard error: minus 186.99 over 147.33, which is minus 1.269.

141
00:31:09,272 --> 00:31:24,760
Step 4: the absolute value, 1.269, is smaller than 1.967, the t quantile with 319 degrees of freedom. The p-value is 0.2053.

142
00:31:25,210 --> 00:31:37,370
Step 5: we do not reject the null hypothesis. The data do not show that the relationship between salary and experience differs between districts of size 1 and 2.

143
00:31:37,820 --> 00:31:49,212
Tutorial 3, question 1 g. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: with card A, the slope in size 3 districts is beta 3 plus beta 5.

144
00:31:49,662 --> 00:31:58,067
Step 2: one combination, so we give a confidence interval: the estimate plus or minus t times the standard error.

145
00:31:58,517 --> 00:32:11,445
Step 3: the estimate is 662.79 minus 518.91, which is 143.88 dollars per year of experience.

146
00:32:11,895 --> 00:32:33,549
The variance comes from the vcov output: 3027.409 plus 24849.737 plus 2 times minus 3027.409. That is 21822.328.

147
00:32:33,999 --> 00:32:50,319
The standard error is 147.7238. The t quantile with 319 degrees of freedom is 1.967428. The margin is 290.636.

148
00:32:50,769 --> 00:33:06,854
Step 4: the interval is 143.88 plus or minus 290.636: minus 146.756 to 434.516.

149
00:33:07,304 --> 00:33:22,835
Step 5: in size 3 districts, the estimated mean salary rises by 143.88 dollars per year of experience. The interval contains 0, so the data do not show a clear association.

150
00:33:23,285 --> 00:33:34,549
Tutorial 3, question 1 h. The width of an interval is 2 times t times the standard error. The standard error stays the same, so only t changes.

151
00:33:34,999 --> 00:33:55,351
A 90 percent interval uses the 0.95 quantile: 1.649644. The 95 percent interval uses the 0.975 quantile: 1.967428. The blue bar is the 90 percent interval.

152
00:33:55,801 --> 00:34:06,425
The ratio is 1.649644 over 1.967428, which is 0.8385.

153
00:34:06,875 --> 00:34:14,747
So the 90 percent interval is narrower. It is 0.84 times the width of the 95 percent interval.

154
00:34:15,197 --> 00:34:28,871
Section 4, question type C. Sometimes a straight line does not fit the data well. These points follow a curve. The gray dashed line misses the trend. The picture uses made-up numbers.

155
00:34:29,321 --> 00:34:38,388
One way to make the model more flexible is to add a quadratic term, beta 2 x i squared. The green curve is a parabola.

156
00:34:38,838 --> 00:34:51,979
The model is still a linear regression model, because it is linear in the betas. X gets one more column, x i squared. So beta hat, the t tests and the F tests all work as before.

157
00:34:52,429 --> 00:34:57,293
Method card C solves the questions of type C. It has four steps.

158
00:34:57,743 --> 00:35:08,111
Step 1: add the columns x i squared, and maybe x i cubed, to X. The model stays linear in beta, so we fit and test it as usual.

159
00:35:08,561 --> 00:35:27,057
Step 2: the effect of x changes with x. From x to x plus 1, the mean changes by beta 1 plus beta 2 times 2 x plus 1. The orange tangents show two slopes. The top of the curve is at minus beta 1 over 2 beta 2.

160
00:35:27,507 --> 00:35:36,937
Step 3: compare models. Test the extra terms. For one term use a t test, and for several terms an F test, as in card B.

161
00:35:37,387 --> 00:35:47,072
Step 4: keep the model hierarchical. With x cubed, keep x squared and x. With x 1 times x 2, keep x 1 and x 2.

162
00:35:47,522 --> 00:35:59,319
The tortoise data have 18 measurements. NumEggs is the number of eggs in the hatch of a tortoise. CarapDiam is the diameter of its carapace, the shell.

163
00:35:59,769 --> 00:36:08,623
The table shows the first six rows from slide 24. Row 1: a diameter of 284 and 3 eggs.

164
00:36:09,073 --> 00:36:19,825
The plot shows all 18 points. The number of eggs goes up at first. Then it goes down for the largest tortoises. A straight line cannot follow this.

165
00:36:20,275 --> 00:36:44,253
Slide 25 fits the quadratic model: step 1 of card C. Here is its coefficient table. The rows are beta 0, beta 1 and beta 2. The estimates are minus 899.9, 5.857, and minus 0.009425. The letter e means a power of 10.

166
00:36:44,703 --> 00:37:06,847
Each t value is the estimate over its standard error. For the squared term, minus 0.009425 over 0.002829 is minus 3.332. The last column is the two-sided p-value of the t test of that coefficient equal to 0.

167
00:37:07,297 --> 00:37:18,497
The residual standard error is sigma hat, 2.671. Its degrees of freedom are n minus p plus 1: 18 minus 3, which is 15.

168
00:37:18,947 --> 00:37:31,598
The first part of the output summarizes the residuals: the minimum, the first quartile, the median, the third quartile, and the maximum. The orange dots are the 18 residuals.

169
00:37:32,048 --> 00:37:35,845
Slide 26 shows the two fits. Here are the data.

170
00:37:36,295 --> 00:37:40,775
The gray dashed line is the straight line fit. It is almost flat.

171
00:37:41,225 --> 00:37:47,988
The green curve is the quadratic fit. It goes up and then down. It follows the data much better.

172
00:37:48,438 --> 00:38:00,299
Slide 27 asks: what is beta 1 here. Pause the video and try it. In the quadratic model, the change in the mean for one more unit of x depends on x.

173
00:38:00,749 --> 00:38:06,147
Step 2 of card C. Write the mean at x plus 1 minus the mean at x.

174
00:38:06,597 --> 00:38:15,343
Expand x plus 1 squared. The change is beta 1 plus beta 2 times 2 x plus 1. It depends on x.

175
00:38:15,793 --> 00:38:21,724
As an aid, the slope of the tangent at x is beta 1 plus 2 beta 2 x.

176
00:38:22,174 --> 00:38:45,086
At a diameter of 295: 2 times 0.009425 times 295 is 5.561. 5.857 minus 5.561 is plus 0.296. Here larger tortoises have more eggs on average.

177
00:38:45,536 --> 00:38:57,440
At 325, the slope is 5.857 minus 6.126, which is minus 0.269. Here the mean goes down.

178
00:38:57,890 --> 00:39:07,746
The top of the curve is where the slope is 0: x equals minus beta 1 hat over 2 beta 2 hat, which is 310.7.

179
00:39:08,196 --> 00:39:25,369
Put x equal to 0: the slope is beta 1. So beta 1 alone is the slope at a diameter of 0. That is far outside the data, 284 to 334, so beta 1 alone has no useful meaning here.

180
00:39:25,819 --> 00:39:41,499
Slide 27 also asks how to test whether the quadratic model is more appropriate than the straight line. Pause the video and try it. Step 3 of card C: the line is the quadratic model with beta 2 equal to 0.

181
00:39:41,949 --> 00:39:52,744
So test the null hypothesis beta 2 equals 0. This is one term, so a t test, with 18 minus 3, which is 15, degrees of freedom.

182
00:39:53,194 --> 00:40:09,322
From the output, t is minus 3.332. Its absolute value is larger than 2.131, the t quantile with 15 degrees of freedom. The p-value is 0.00455.

183
00:40:09,772 --> 00:40:15,105
We reject the null hypothesis. The quadratic model fits better than the line.

184
00:40:15,555 --> 00:40:28,611
Tutorial 3, question 1, parts i to k. Pause the video and try it. Part i: write the fitted model. Here s 2 and s 3 are the size indicators, and x is experience.

185
00:40:29,061 --> 00:40:38,363
Is it a linear model? Yes. Step 1 of card C: it is linear in the betas. X only gets one more column, the squared experience.

186
00:40:38,813 --> 00:40:56,775
Part j: step 3 of card C. The test of beta 4 equal to 0 is a t test. t is minus 6.124 over 11.409, which is minus 0.537. The p-value is 0.59.

187
00:40:57,225 --> 00:41:09,044
We do not reject. There is not enough evidence of a quadratic relationship. This does not prove that the relationship is linear, and the test checks only one kind of curve.

188
00:41:09,494 --> 00:41:25,942
Part k: beta 3 equal to beta 4 equal to 0 means no association of mean salary with experience. The alternative is a linear or quadratic association. Two betas together, so it is an F test.

189
00:41:26,392 --> 00:41:40,067
Part l. Pause the video and try it. With the squared term, beta 3 is the change at experience 0 only. So beta 3 alone has no simple meaning, and it is not useful to explain.

190
00:41:40,517 --> 00:41:50,821
Step 2 of card C: from x to x plus 1 years, the mean salary changes by beta 3 hat plus beta 4 hat times 2 x plus 1.

191
00:41:51,271 --> 00:42:10,321
From 10 to 11 years: 6.124 times 21 is 128.604. 730.652 minus 128.604 is 602.048 dollars.

192
00:42:10,771 --> 00:42:24,915
From 20 to 21 years: 6.124 times 41 is 251.084. The change is 479.568 dollars.

193
00:42:25,365 --> 00:42:36,949
So two ways to explain it: one, plot the fitted curve, here for size 1 districts. Two, give the change for one more year at chosen values of experience.

194
00:42:37,399 --> 00:42:53,869
Beyond polynomial terms, linear regression can use any known functions of x. The model is the sum of beta j times f j of x i. Each f j is a known function, for example x, x squared, or log x.

195
00:42:54,319 --> 00:43:10,084
It is easy to add too many terms. Then the model can overfit: the curve follows the random noise in this sample, and not the true mean. The red curve uses all powers of x up to 7. It is computed from the data.

196
00:43:10,534 --> 00:43:18,385
More terms also make the coefficients harder to interpret. So there is a trade-off between fit and interpretability.

197
00:43:18,835 --> 00:43:38,931
Step 4 of card C is the hierarchical principle of slide 29. Fit hierarchically well-formulated models: if the model has a higher-order term, it also has all the lower-order terms under it. With x 1 times x 2, include the main effects x 1 and x 2.

198
00:43:39,381 --> 00:43:47,381
With the three-way product x 1 x 2 x 3, include all three two-way products, and all three main effects.

199
00:43:47,831 --> 00:43:56,129
The same rule holds for polynomial terms. With x cubed, include x squared and x. The next slide shows why.

200
00:43:56,579 --> 00:44:08,377
Slide 30 takes a model with x squared but no x term. Its curve must have its lowest point at x equal to 0, if beta 2 is positive. The black curve.

201
00:44:08,827 --> 00:44:19,365
Now shift x by a fixed amount b. For example, center x at its mean, so b is x bar. The blue curve is the same curve, moved by b.

202
00:44:19,815 --> 00:44:27,730
Expand the square. x i minus b, squared, is x i squared, minus 2 x i b, plus b squared.

203
00:44:28,180 --> 00:44:39,103
Collect the powers of x. The model now has a linear term, minus 2 b beta 2, times x i, in orange. It appears only because of the shift.

204
00:44:39,553 --> 00:44:49,878
So the model without the x term changes its form after a change of origin. A hierarchical model, with x and x squared, keeps its form.

205
00:44:50,328 --> 00:45:10,104
Practice question 1 uses the tortoise data. Part a: fit Model A, the straight line, and test beta 1 equals 0 at the 5 percent level. Pause the video and try it. With card B: one coefficient, so a t test with 18 minus 2, which is 16, degrees of freedom.

206
00:45:10,554 --> 00:45:23,098
Computed from the data, beta 1 hat is 0.0276, with a standard error of 0.0563. t is 0.490.

207
00:45:23,548 --> 00:45:42,577
The t quantile with 16 degrees of freedom is 2.120. The absolute value of t is smaller, and the p-value is 0.631. We do not reject the null hypothesis. The line shows no evidence of a straight-line association.

208
00:45:43,027 --> 00:45:58,451
Part b: fit Model B, the quadratic model, and test beta 1 equals beta 2 equals 0. Pause the video and try it. Two terms together, so an F test with q equal to 2 and 15 degrees of freedom.

209
00:45:58,901 --> 00:46:17,056
Computed from the data, F is 5.747. The 95 percent quantile of F with 2 and 15 degrees of freedom is 3.682. The p-value is 0.0140. We reject the null hypothesis.

210
00:46:17,506 --> 00:46:31,991
This does not conflict with part a. Part a looks only for a straight-line trend. The mean goes up and then down, so the best line is almost flat. Model B can follow the curve, so it finds the association.

211
00:46:32,441 --> 00:46:44,345
Part c: fit Model C, which adds x cubed, and test beta 3 equals 0. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card C: X gets the column x cubed.

212
00:46:44,795 --> 00:47:02,566
Step 3: one term, so a t test with 18 minus 4, which is 14, degrees of freedom. Computed from the data, t is 0.613. The t quantile is 2.145, and the p-value is 0.550.

213
00:47:03,016 --> 00:47:14,813
We do not reject the null hypothesis. The purple dashed cubic curve is almost on the green quadratic curve. So Model C is not more appropriate than Model B.

214
00:47:15,263 --> 00:47:42,442
Part d: in Model C, test beta 2 equals beta 3 equals 0. Pause the video and try it. Two terms, so an F test with q equal to 2 and 14 degrees of freedom. Computed from the data, F is 5.508. The 95 percent quantile is 3.739. The p-value is 0.0172. We reject the null hypothesis.

215
00:47:42,892 --> 00:48:02,049
This does not conflict with part c. Part c asks if x cubed adds to x and x squared. Part d asks if x squared and x cubed together add to x. Also, every single t value in Model C is below 0.75 in absolute value.

216
00:48:02,499 --> 00:48:25,198
The plot shows the reason. On the range 284 to 334, x squared and x cubed lie almost on a straight line. Their correlation is 0.9997. So they carry almost the same information, and one of them adds little when the other is in the model. A later lecture studies this problem.

217
00:48:25,648 --> 00:48:43,461
Part e: fit Model D, with x and x cubed, but no x squared. Test beta 2 equals 0. Pause the video and try it. In Model D, beta 2 is the coefficient of x cubed. One term, so a t test with 15 degrees of freedom.

218
00:48:43,911 --> 00:49:05,181
Computed from the data, t is minus 3.317, and the p-value is 0.0047. We reject the null hypothesis. The teal curve is close to the green curve, and sigma hat is 2.676, against 2.671 for Model B.

219
00:49:05,631 --> 00:49:16,276
Step 4 of card C: Model D has x cubed and not x squared, so it is not hierarchical. The hierarchical principle chooses Model B.

220
00:49:16,726 --> 00:49:33,771
To recap. Card A interprets an interaction model. Fix the group, collect the terms into an intercept and a slope, and name each beta. A main effect is an intercept difference. An interaction is a slope difference: a difference in differences.

221
00:49:34,221 --> 00:49:49,496
Card B tests an effect. Write it as one linear combination, for a t test or a confidence interval, or as several betas equal to 0, for an F test. The standard error comes from the vcov output.

222
00:49:49,946 --> 00:50:04,687
Card C handles curves. Add x squared to X: the model stays linear. The effect of x then depends on x. Test the extra terms, and keep the lower-order terms. This is the end of video 20.
